Substituting a worker with below average permanent productivity with a worker with above average permanent productivity is associated with a 1 percent increase in the effort of other workers on the same shift.
This positive spillover suggests that positive peer effects dominate free-riding.


The magnitude of the spillover effect appears to vary dramatically depending on the skill levelof the relevant worker.

