@phdthesis{gomez_mentor_2009,
	title = {Mentor and protégé core competencies in the {Army} {Officer} {Corps}: {A} qualitative study to align the mentoring process with current operations - {ProQuest}},
	shorttitle = {Mentor and protégé core competencies in the {Army} {Officer} {Corps}},
	url = {https://www.proquest.com/openview/c830742004f7abbee6a87b9ed82047b9/1/advanced},
	abstract = {ABSTRACT
     The U.S. Army’s mentorship process is an informal process that does not define vital mentor and protégé core competencies required for a successful relationship. For this reason, it is possible that mentoring relationships either do not last or have less than optimal results. Today’s Army is a more flexible force capable of simultaneously upholding diverse battlefronts and other peace-keeping missions. Therefore, its mentoring process may require a paradigm shift to meet the new mission requirements. With leaders spread across diverse locations and with equally diverse missions, affording them the professional development necessary to succeed is vital. 
     This study took advantage of the strengths of the qualitative methodology, employing the Delphi technique, exercised on a homogeneous group. The research herein was anticipated to result in a grounded theory determined from the responses of a panel of experts in the field of Army leadership. The expert panel was composed of Army
colonels (O6) from a variety of basic branches. This study collected a series of core competencies vital in mentors and protégés and provided by the panel members. The   research was conducted by means of cyber-dissemination of questionnaires.
     The focus of this study was to identify the core competencies that Army senior leaders desire in both mentors and protégés that would possibly help mentoring relationships succeed in the U.S. Army, as well as determine whether or not panel members would agree with the implementation of a formal mentoring program. The panel members provided 54 mentor competencies and 48 protégé competencies which were subsequently narrowed down to the top ten for each category. The top twenty competencies served as basis for the creation of an Army mentorship framework model.
     With the identification of mentor and protégé competencies the path to a tailored mentorship program has been highlighted. Having such pertinent information can aid Army leadership in establishing a program that can positively affect mission accomplishment by developing officers capable of carrying out all tasks and duties assigned. There is still ground to be covered in order to make such a program work and future research needs to occur prior to any planning and implementation processes.},
	language = {en},
	urldate = {2021-07-28},
	school = {Capella University},
	author = {Gomez, Elliott},
	month = aug,
	year = {2009},
	file = {Mentor and protégé core competencies in the Army O.pdf:files/256/Mentor and protégé core competencies in the Army O.pdf:application/pdf;Snapshot:files/244/advanced.html:text/html},
}

